
This directory is intended for PlatformIO Test Runner and project tests.

Unit Testing is a software testing method by which individual units of
source code, sets of one or more MCU program modules together with associated
control data, usage procedures, and operating procedures, are tested to
determine whether they are fit for use. Unit testing finds problems early
in the development cycle.

More information about PlatformIO Unit Testing:
- https://docs.platformio.org/en/latest/advanced/unit-testing/index.html

## Native pairing-state tests

Run the pure C++ pairing-state tests with PlatformIO Core 6.1.19:

```powershell
$pioExe = Join-Path $env:USERPROFILE '.platformio\penv\Scripts\pio.exe'
& $pioExe test -e native -f test_pairing_state
```

On Windows, use a C++17-capable MinGW compiler. The older optional
`toolchain-gccmingw32` package provides GCC 5.1 and lacks `std::optional`.
The portable compiler used for validation is the x64 UCRT GCC 16.2.0 release
from [WinLibs](https://github.com/brechtsanders/winlibs_mingw/releases/tag/16.2.0posix-14.0.0-ucrt-r2),
with archive SHA-256 `53fb6773fbdf87cdf22b69a6b2a07ee33d3ada413b42d83044bc7cc6481400d2`.
After extracting it under `.pio/native-tools/winlibs-16.2.0-ucrt-r2`, run the
command in PowerShell with its compiler directory temporarily first on `PATH`:

```powershell
$pairingTestOldPath = $env:PATH
$pairingTestOldEncoding = $env:PYTHONIOENCODING
try {
    $nativeCompilerBin = (Resolve-Path .pio/native-tools/winlibs-16.2.0-ucrt-r2/mingw64/bin).Path
    $pioExe = Join-Path $env:USERPROFILE '.platformio\penv\Scripts\pio.exe'
    $env:PATH = $nativeCompilerBin + ';' + $pairingTestOldPath
    $env:PYTHONIOENCODING = 'utf-8'
    & $pioExe test -e native -f test_pairing_state
} finally {
    $env:PATH = $pairingTestOldPath
    if ($null -eq $pairingTestOldEncoding) {
        Remove-Item Env:PYTHONIOENCODING -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
    } else {
        $env:PYTHONIOENCODING = $pairingTestOldEncoding
    }
}
```

## Direct web-validation tests

The standalone native web-validation test currently runs 71 checks. With the WinLibs
toolchain extracted under `.pio/native-tools/winlibs-16.2.0-ucrt-r2`, compile
and run it directly from the repository root:

```powershell
$webValidationOldPath = $env:PATH
try {
    $nativeCompilerBin = (Resolve-Path .pio/native-tools/winlibs-16.2.0-ucrt-r2/mingw64/bin).Path
    New-Item -ItemType Directory -Path .pio/test-results -Force | Out-Null
    $webValidationTest = Join-Path (Resolve-Path .pio/test-results).Path 'web-validation-tests.exe'
    $env:PATH = $nativeCompilerBin + ';' + $webValidationOldPath
    & (Join-Path $nativeCompilerBin 'g++.exe') -std=c++17 -Wall -Wextra -Werror -Isrc `
        .\test\test_web_validation\test_main.cpp .\src\webValidation.cpp -o $webValidationTest
    if ($LASTEXITCODE -ne 0) { throw 'Web-validation test compilation failed.' }
    & $webValidationTest
    if ($LASTEXITCODE -ne 0) { throw 'Web-validation tests failed.' }
} finally {
    $env:PATH = $webValidationOldPath
}
```

The executable prints the individual results and a pass/fail total. The 71 C++
checks exercise pure validation helpers. They are software checks;
they do not exercise the device HTTP server, prove HTTP security behavior, or
verify BLE subscription delivery.

The source-owned extended-advertising transition contract also has a read-only
static regression. It verifies that explicit C3 stop completion uses the
successful NimBLE stop return plus inactive state, while spontaneous
ADV_COMPLETE callbacks remain distinct. Run it from PowerShell 7 before a C3 build:

```powershell
pwsh -NoProfile -File .\test\verify-advertising-stop-completion.ps1
```

## Descriptor fixture validation

Before running the repeatable descriptor-fixture matrix for startup, GATT,
advertising, or status-serialization changes, run these executable native
regressions in order:

```powershell
python test/descriptor_fixture_native/run.py
python test/descriptor_startup_native/run.py
python test/web_status_native/run.py
```

The fixture regression runs seven cases against the actual
`src/fixtureGatt.cpp`, checks contracts in the pinned installed NimBLE source,
and proves a known-bad wrong-enum variant fails the normal success case. The
startup regression runs six cases across legacy and extended advertising
configurations. It compiles the actual fixture source and extracts the current
production startup and advertising setup bodies, `GetConfig`/`TrySnapshot`, and
the readiness-gated rebuild guard. Their native outputs use independent
folders:
`.pio/descriptor-fixture/native-regression` and
`.pio/descriptor-fixture/native-startup`.

The web-status regression extracts the production `operationDataJson`,
`operationRecordJson`, `statusIsComplete`, `hexValue`, `getStatus`, and `getGatt`
bodies against installed cJSON. Its two executables are the current code and
known-bad status-validator/unchecked-target-allocation variants, both in one
native legacy-advertising configuration; they are not separate C3 and WROVER
builds. The harness supplies a three-target `FixtureGatt::GetValues()` snapshot
stub, `makeStatus`, and HTTP-response stubs. It checks all 11 GATT snapshot
fields, including the minimum connected-peer MTU and peer count, plus hex values.
Status-envelope fixtures require a numeric firmware revision, a full 64-character
hex source ID, and a nonempty environment string; malformed or wrongly typed
identity values are rejected.

The cJSON hook injects failure at each allocation observed in the fixture and
displays the discovered allocation count for that run. Explicit snapshot `Failed`
returns HTTP 500. `Busy` and `NotReady` return HTTP 200 with
`valuesAvailable:false` and a null `values` field; unavailable service inventory
is also represented as null. The GATT route accepts `publishValue`, `addService`,
`removeService`, and `sendServiceChanged`; other routes are unchanged. The harness
does not run production `GetValues()`, the full `makeStatus` snapshot producer,
live HTTP routes or authentication, or device BLE behavior. Generated native
artifacts are retained under `.pio/web-status/native`. When invoked by the
validation matrix, the runner's stdout and stderr are captured in the unique run
directory; a direct invocation does not write a separate log file. Run it directly
with:

Run it directly with:

```powershell
python test/web_status_native/run.py
```

Run the read-only fixture source safety check:

```powershell
python test/verify-descriptor-fixture.py
```

Run the repeatable validation matrix:

```powershell
pwsh -NoProfile -File tools/validate-descriptor-fixture.ps1
```

Do not run PlatformIO commands concurrently when they share a `.pio` build tree. These
executable checks complement the static source, browser, and pure software checks. They
do not establish radio or over-the-air behavior, device HTTP/security behavior,
central-side descriptor or subscription acceptance, or physical-device acceptance;
record those separately with the device acceptance checklist.

## Browser control regression checks

Run the inline browser script against the focused Node DOM stub:

```powershell
node .\test\web_ui_regression.cjs
```

This checks polling synchronization, preserved edits, worker feedback, pairing
controls, firmware identity fallback, GATT byte parsing and publication bounds,
peer MTU and cap explanations, scenario prefill, no-peer and stale-snapshot
messages, and removal of the Restore action in a Node DOM stub. It does not test
browser layout, live device HTTP/security behavior, central descriptor
operations, or actual subscription delivery.

## Dynamic GATT rebuild feasibility harness

Run from the feature branch root:

~~~
python test/dynamic_gatt_native/run.py
python test/dynamic_entry_native/run.py
python test/dynamic_recovery_native/run.py
python test/dynamic_topology_native/run.py
python test/dynamic_advertising_native/run.py
~~~

The runner reads NimBLE-Arduino 2.5.1 from the original repository's C3 and WROVER
PlatformIO dependency trees, checks installed versions and source hashes, extracts
the pinned ATT reset, GATT count/reset/stop and visibility functions plus the server
remove wrapper, and compiles those bodies with production `src/gattRebuild.h`.
It checks mutation-before-reset ordering, ATT-list clearing while the pool is live,
capacity clearing on stop, and 128 rebuild cycles bounded by current topology. It
injects precondition, remove, reset, count, allocation, registration, and verification
failures. A second executable compiles and runs the exact pinned `addService` and
`start` bodies: a nonremoved service is appended without marking GATT changed, then
`start()` returns true without resetting or registering it. The same executable
checks that re-adding a removed service takes the reset-and-register path. The runner
also compiles the extracted production UUID normalization, protected-identity, and
add-input validation bodies against fixture UUIDs from `src/fixtureGatt.h`.

The lifecycle adapter supplies mutability, allocation, registration, and lookup
stubs rather than the full NimBLEServer start/resetGATT dependency path. The separate
wrapper executable uses reset and registration stubs around the extracted wrapper
bodies. These are deterministic source-level feasibility checks, not proof of live
multi-task safety or physical device behavior. Host-event serialization, application
exclusion, bonded CCCD handle
checks, and device-reported revision/fingerprint acceptance remain separate gates.
Generated compiler inputs and executables are retained under
`.pio/dynamic-gatt-native/<run-id>`.

The entry runner executes production entry leasing, fixture readiness/CAS admission,
and queued-host cancellation/lifetime code. The recovery runner executes the
production structural transaction and lifecycle adapter with injected unchanged,
uncertain, registration, verification, and restoration outcomes. The advertising
runner compiles production capture/restore, stop, timer, deferred callback, and
serial admission bodies in both legacy and extended configurations. It exercises
host truth, inactive configurations, pending restart intent/generations, timer
allocation/start failures, stale callback replay, D/S exclusion, Q/W availability,
and buffered advertising submission. These constrained adapters do not establish
live radio behavior or hardware task-stack headroom.

The topology runner executes the production fixture identity capture/verification
against native lookup adapters, including GAP, Service Changed, every fixture CCCD,
characteristic values, descriptor metadata, ownership, and missing/moved handles.
